1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,110
Listen to part of a lecture in a college music class.

2
00:00:03,110 --> 00:00:06,010
OK... That about does it for today.

3
00:00:06,010 --> 00:00:10,190
We're going to spend the next few classes on a different kind of music.

4
00:00:10,190 --> 00:00:17,050
We'll examine the development of jazz from its beginnings right on up to the latest in jazz music.

5
00:00:17,050 --> 00:00:23,598
We'll look at jazz as the most successful music indigenous to the United States... born in New Orleans

6
00:00:23,598 --> 00:00:26,010
and raised in Chicago and New York.

7
00:00:26,010 --> 00:00:29,443
We'll begin with the African roots of the music

8
00:00:29,443 --> 00:00:34,250
and how African rhythms blended with Western music to create an entirely new form.

9
00:00:34,250 --> 00:00:37,260
Then we'll follow jazz into its heyday

10
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:40,130
the 1920's, which was known as the Jazz Age.

11
00:00:40,130 --> 00:00:43,080
It was in this era that the blues were born.

12
00:00:43,080 --> 00:00:47,620
The 1930's saw the evolution of "swing

13
00:00:47,620 --> 00:00:51,160
jazz music played by the big bands like that of Tommy Dorsey.

14
00:00:51,160 --> 00:00:53,328
If there's any generalization that can made about jazz,

15
00:00:53,328 --> 00:00:57,340
it's that the music is always changing

16
00:00:57,680 --> 00:00:58,532
evolving... from the Bebop of the '40's to the "cool jazz" of the '50's

17
00:00:58,532 --> 00:01:00,050
and on into the experimental '60's.

18
00:01:00,050 --> 00:01:09,220
The '70's saw jazz brought into the electronic age by artists like Herbie Hancock.

19
00:01:09,220 --> 00:01:14,702
We'll end up listening to some examples of the newest transformation of jazz... Afro-pop

20
00:01:14,702 --> 00:01:21,280
and the Latin beats that have brought new international flair to this music born in the United States.

21
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:25,190
Be sure to read chapters 13 and 14 of your text.

22
00:01:25,190 --> 00:01:27,060
They'll get you started.

23
00:01:27,060 --> 00:01:29,113
If there are any jazz devotees among you,

24
00:01:29,113 --> 00:01:33,220
feel free to bring in your favorite tapes or CD's to share with the class.

25
00:01:33,220 --> 00:01:38,220
Renee Rosnes' latest release would be great if anyone has it.
